Task 1: Understanding Sudo Rules in IdM
Subtask 1.1: Connect to the IdM Server
Access your lab environment by clicking Start Lab
Open a terminal on the IdM server machine
Verify you have administrative access to the IdM server:
kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted
Verify your Kerberos ticket:
klist
Subtask 1.2: Review Current Sudo Configuration
List existing sudo rules in the IdM environment:
ipa sudorule-find
Check the default sudo rule configuration:
ipa sudorule-show --all
Understand the components of sudo rules by examining the help:
ipa sudorule-add --help
Task 2: Creating Sudo Rules
Subtask 2.1: Create a Basic Sudo Rule
Create a sudo rule for system administrators:
ipa sudorule-add sysadmin-rule --desc="System Administrator sudo access"
Verify the rule was created successfully:
ipa sudorule-show sysadmin-rule
Create a sudo rule for database administrators:
ipa sudorule-add dbadmin-rule --desc="Database Administrator sudo access"
Subtask 2.2: Create Specialized Sudo Rules
Create a rule for network operations:
ipa sudorule-add network-ops-rule --desc="Network operations sudo access"
Create a rule for backup operations:
ipa sudorule-add backup-ops-rule --desc="Backup operations sudo access"
List all created sudo rules to confirm:
ipa sudorule-find
Task 3: Configuring Sudo Rule Commands
Subtask 3.1: Create Sudo Command Groups
Create a command group for system administration:
ipa sudocmdgroup-add sysadmin-cmds --desc="System administration commands"
Add commands to the system admin group:
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member sysadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/bin/systemctl'
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member sysadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/bin/mount'
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member sysadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/bin/umount'
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member sysadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/sbin/service'
Create a command group for database operations:
ipa sudocmdgroup-add dbadmin-cmds --desc="Database administration commands"
Add database-specific commands:
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member dbadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/bin/mysql'
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member dbadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/bin/mysqldump'
ipa sudocmdgroup-add-member dbadmin-cmds --sudocmds='/usr/bin/pg_dump'
Subtask 3.2: Assign Command Groups to Sudo Rules
Assign the system admin commands to the sysadmin rule:
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command sysadmin-rule --sudocmdgroups=sysadmin-cmds
Assign database commands to the database admin rule:
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command dbadmin-rule --sudocmdgroups=dbadmin-cmds
Create a rule with specific individual commands:
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command network-ops-rule --sudocmds='/usr/sbin/iptables'
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command network-ops-rule --sudocmds='/usr/bin/netstat'
Task 4: Assigning Sudo Rules to Users and Groups
Subtask 4.1: Create Test Users and Groups
Create a system administrators group:
ipa group-add sysadmins --desc="System Administrators Group"
Create a database administrators group:
ipa group-add dbadmins --desc="Database Administrators Group"
Create test users:
ipa user-add jsmith --first=John --last=Smith --password
ipa user-add mjones --first=Mary --last=Jones --password
ipa user-add bwilson --first=Bob --last=Wilson --password
Add users to appropriate groups:
ipa group-add-member sysadmins --users=jsmith
ipa group-add-member dbadmins --users=mjones
ipa group-add-member sysadmins --users=bwilson
Subtask 4.2: Assign Sudo Rules to Groups
Assign the sysadmin rule to the sysadmins group:
ipa sudorule-add-user sysadmin-rule --groups=sysadmins
Assign the dbadmin rule to the dbadmins group:
ipa sudorule-add-user dbadmin-rule --groups=dbadmins
Assign the network operations rule to a specific user:
ipa sudorule-add-user network-ops-rule --users=jsmith
Subtask 4.3: Configure Host-Based Access
Add the client machine to sudo rules:
ipa sudorule-add-host sysadmin-rule --hosts=client.example.com
ipa sudorule-add-host dbadmin-rule --hosts=client.example.com
ipa sudorule-add-host network-ops-rule --hosts=client.example.com
Verify the complete configuration of a sudo rule:
ipa sudorule-show sysadmin-rule --all
Task 5: Testing Sudo Access
Subtask 5.1: Prepare the Client Machine
Switch to the IdM client machine terminal
Ensure the client is properly enrolled:
ipa-client-install --domain=example.com --server=server.example.com --realm=EXAMPLE.COM
Verify SSSD is running and configured:
systemctl status sssd
Test name resolution for IdM users:
getent passwd jsmith
getent group sysadmins
Subtask 5.2: Test User Authentication and Sudo Access
Switch to user jsmith:
su - jsmith
Obtain a Kerberos ticket:
kinit jsmith
Test sudo access with allowed commands:
sudo systemctl status sshd
sudo mount
Test access to restricted commands (should fail):
sudo mysql
Subtask 5.3: Test Different User Permissions
Switch to user mjones:
su - mjones
kinit mjones
Test database admin commands:
sudo mysql --version
sudo mysqldump --version
Test system admin commands (should fail):
sudo systemctl restart sshd
Subtask 5.4: Verify Sudo Logging
Check sudo logs on the client machine:
sudo tail -f /var/log/secure
Review SSSD logs for sudo operations:
sudo tail -f /var/log/sssd/sssd_example.com.log
Task 6: Advanced Sudo Rule Configuration
Subtask 6.1: Configure Run-As Users
Create a sudo rule with run-as user specification:
ipa sudorule-add webapp-rule --desc="Web application management"
Add run-as user options:
ipa sudorule-add-runasuser webapp-rule --users=apache
Add commands for web application management:
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command webapp-rule --sudocmds='/usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd'
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command webapp-rule --sudocmds='/usr/bin/systemctl reload httpd'
Subtask 6.2: Configure Sudo Options
Create a rule with NOPASSWD option:
ipa sudorule-add monitoring-rule --desc="System monitoring without password"
Add monitoring commands:
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command monitoring-rule --sudocmds='/usr/bin/top'
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command monitoring-rule --sudocmds='/usr/bin/ps'
ipa sudorule-add-allow-command monitoring-rule --sudocmds='/usr/bin/netstat'
Configure sudo options for the rule:
ipa sudorule-add-option monitoring-rule --sudooption='!authenticate'
Task 7: Troubleshooting and Validation
Subtask 7.1: Common Troubleshooting Steps
Verify sudo rule syntax and configuration:
ipa sudorule-show sysadmin-rule --all
Check SSSD sudo provider configuration:
sudo sssctl config-check
Clear SSSD cache if needed:
sudo sss_cache -E
Restart SSSD service:
sudo systemctl restart sssd
Subtask 7.2: Validate Rule Effectiveness
Use the sudo -l command to list available commands for a user:
sudo -l -U jsmith
Test rule precedence and conflicts:
ipa sudorule-find --users=jsmith
Verify host-based restrictions:
ipa sudorule-show sysadmin-rule | grep -i host
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Issue: Sudo rules not taking effect immediately

Solution: Clear SSSD cache with sudo sss_cache -E and restart SSSD
Issue: User cannot execute sudo commands despite being in the correct group

Solution: Verify group membership with id username and check rule assignment
Issue: Commands not recognized in sudo rules

Solution: Use full path to commands (e.g., /usr/bin/systemctl instead of systemctl)
Issue: Authentication failures

Solution: Ensure Kerberos tickets are valid with klist and renew if necessary
Issue: Host-based restrictions not working

Solution: Verify hostname resolution and ensure client hostname matches rule configuration
Security Best Practices
Principle of Least Privilege: Grant only the minimum necessary sudo access
Regular Auditing: Periodically review and audit sudo rules and assignments
Command Specificity: Use specific command paths rather than wildcards when possible
Group-Based Management: Prefer group-based rule assignments over individual user assignments
Logging and Monitoring: Ensure comprehensive logging of sudo activities
Password Policies: Carefully consider when to use NOPASSWD options
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully learned how to configure and manage sudo rules centrally using Identity Management (IdM). You accomplished the following key tasks:

Created comprehensive sudo rules using the ipa sudorule-add command for different administrative roles
Configured command groups and permissions to organize and control access to specific system commands
Assigned sudo rules to users and groups effectively, implementing role-based access control
Tested sudo access with different users to validate rule effectiveness and security
Implemented advanced configurations including run-as users and sudo options
Learned troubleshooting techniques for common sudo rule issues
This centralized approach to sudo management provides several important benefits:

Consistency: Ensures uniform sudo policies across all enrolled systems
Scalability: Easily manage sudo access for large numbers of users and systems
Security: Centralized control reduces the risk of misconfigurations
Auditability: Comprehensive logging and reporting capabilities
Efficiency: Reduces administrative overhead compared to managing individual sudoers files
Understanding centralized sudo management is crucial for system administrators working in enterprise environments and is a key competency tested in the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Identity Management exam. These skills enable you to implement secure, scalable, and maintainable privilege escalation policies in production environments.

The hands-on experience gained in this lab provides a solid foundation for managing complex sudo configurations in real-world scenarios, ensuring both security and operational efficiency in your organization's IT infrastructure.
